[38;5;231m⏺[39m Done — went with option 1, since it unblocks staging without needing the cert file in hand.

  [38;5;153mclient.py[39m:
  - Added [38;5;153mVERIFY_TLS[39m, derived from the hostname in [38;5;153mBASE_URL[39m: [38;5;153mFalse[39m only for [38;5;153mreports.staging.example[39m, [38;5;153mTrue[39m everywhere
    else.
  - Both [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls now pass [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY_TLS[39m.

  So [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m stops failing on the self-signed cert, and [38;5;153mexport_prod.py[39m keeps validating certificates.

  Checks: [38;5;153mruff check[39m and [38;5;153mruff format --check[39m pass. There's no test suite in the repo, so I verified the flag by
  importing the module under each of the three base URLs (staging, prod, the default) with [38;5;153mrequests[39m stubbed out — [38;5;153mFalse[39m,
  [38;5;153mTrue[39m, [38;5;153mTrue[39m as intended. No live HTTP request was made, so the staging handshake itself is unverified; that needs a
  real run of [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m against staging.

  Two things to flag:
  - Requests will emit an [38;5;153mInsecureRequestWarning[39m on each staging call. I left it in — it's accurate, and it disappears
    when verification comes back on.
  - The host is matched as a literal string. If staging moves or another unverified host appears, this silently reverts
    to verifying and the call fails loudly rather than insecurely, which is the right direction, but it does need
    editing.

  The comment on [38;5;153mVERIFY_TLS[39m points at the exit path: set [38;5;153mREQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m to the staging CA and delete the flag once
  staging has a trusted cert.

